<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · c. 528 BCE · Central &amp; East Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Song of the Yue Boatman</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="und">越人歌</bdi> / <bdi lang="und-Latn">Yuèrén Gē</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A boatman's song from about 2,500 years ago, in a lost language of southern China, written down with Chinese characters.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous Yue boatman</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Yue people of the Yangtze River valley, in the state of Chu</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>an ancient Yue language, transcribed in Chinese characters, Old Chinese (the Chinese version)</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Yangtze River valley, present-day China</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>said to have been recorded around 528 BC; preserved in Liu Xiang's Garden of Stories, five centuries later</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional; said to have been recorded around 528 BC)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-of-the-yue-boatman">Song of the Yue Boatman</a> is a short song in an ancient language of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze River</a> valley, said to have been recorded around 528 BC. It survives in the Garden of Stories (Shuoyuan), compiled by Liu Xiang five centuries later, which preserves both a transcription of the original sounds in Chinese characters and a Chinese version.</p><p>In the Garden of Stories the song sits inside a story within a story. A minister of the state of Chu, infatuated with a handsome nobleman, the Lord of Xiangcheng, tells him of the 6th-century BC prince Zixi, Lord of È. On an excursion on his state barge, Zixi was struck by the singing of his Yue boatman and asked for an interpreter to translate it. The song praised rural life and told of the boatman's secret pleasure at knowing the prince. On hearing it, the prince embraced the boatman and covered him with his embroidered coverlet. The minister points out that Zixi was the Chu king's brother and prime minister, yet a Yue boatman could become close to him; persuaded, the Lord of Xiangcheng lets the minister hold his hand, which he had refused before. The story became an emblem of love between men in imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and Feng Menglong placed it in the chapter on love between men in his anthology History of Love (Qing Shi, c. 1628–1630).</p><p>The original words were written down in 32 Chinese characters, each standing for the sound of a non-Chinese syllable. Because the sounds of Old Chinese that those characters stood for are uncertain, the song is hard to read. In 1981 the linguist Wei Qingwen proposed an interpretation by comparing it with Tai languages, especially the Zhuang varieties spoken today in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a>. Building on his work, Zhengzhang Shangfang produced a version in written Thai of the late 13th century, as the closest available approximation to the original language. Both readings correspond loosely to the 54-character Chinese version but have nothing matching its third and ninth lines, which Zhengzhang suggests were added to fit the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chu-ci">Chu Ci</a> poetic style. His interpretation remains controversial, because nearly two thousand years separate the song from written Thai and because Thai belongs to the more distant Southwestern Tai languages. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> Xiaohang has argued that this kind of transcription, though not a true writing system, formed the basis of the later Sawndip script for Zhuang.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A boatman's song in praise of country life, in which he tells of his secret joy at being in the company of a prince. Its mood is warm, shy and admiring.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">an ancient Yue language, transcribed in Chinese characters</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">an ancient Yue language, transcribed in Chinese characters</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="und">今夕何夕兮，</span><span class="sg-r" lang="und-Latn">jīn xī hé xī xī,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What evening is this evening,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="und">搴舟中流。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="und-Latn">qiān zhōu zhōng liú.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that I pull the boat along mid-stream?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="und">今日何日兮，</span><span class="sg-r" lang="und-Latn">jīn rì hé rì xī,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What day is this day,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="und">得與王子同舟。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="und-Latn">dé yǔ wángzǐ tóng zhōu.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that I may share a boat with the prince?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="und">蒙羞被好兮，</span><span class="sg-r" lang="und-Latn">méng xiū bèi hǎo xī,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Shamed, and yet favoured,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="und">不訾詬恥。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="und-Latn">bù zǐ gòu chǐ.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I do not mind reproach or disgrace.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="und">心幾頑而不絕兮，</span><span class="sg-r" lang="und-Latn">xīn jī wán ér bù jué xī,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My heart is troubled [?] and will not cease,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="und">得知王子。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="und-Latn">dé zhī wángzǐ.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I have come to know the prince.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="und">山有木兮木有枝，</span><span class="sg-r" lang="und-Latn">shān yǒu mù xī mù yǒu zhī,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>On the mountain there are trees, and the trees have branches;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="und">心悅君兮君不知。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="und-Latn">xīn yuè jūn xī jūn bù zhī.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>my heart delights in you, my lord, and you do not know.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional; the Chinese text of Liu Xiang's Shuoyuan, as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Song_of_the_Yue_Boatman" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (pinyin).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (7)</summary><ul><li>兮 (xī) is a sung particle with no meaning of its own; it is not translated.</li><li>2: 搴 is 'to pull, to lift'; the boat is pulled or poled along in the middle of the stream.</li><li>5: 蒙羞被好 is read as 'though shamed (of low station), I am favoured (by the prince)'.</li><li>6: 不訾詬恥, 'not reckoning reproach and shame': I do not mind being thought shameless.</li><li>7: 頑 is disputed: 'foolish', 'stubborn', 'troubled'; 幾 'almost'.</li><li>8: 得知王子 can also be read 'that the prince might know (me)'.</li><li>9-10: 枝 (zhī, branch) sounds like 知 (zhī, to know): the trees have branches, but my lord does not know.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
